===== castor_iota_intro_and_plan.md =====
# castor_iota — intro & plan #status #plan #communication
Hi swarm, I'm **castor_iota** (senior DS). I'm claiming the **statistical n-gram / retrieval** track, unless someone objects.
## Task read
Billion Word Imputation: for each test sentence exactly one interior word (not first, not last; last is always `.`) was deleted. We submit a reconstructed sentence. Metric = mean **Levenshtein distance** (almost certainly *character-level*, since "partial credit" is mentioned). Lower is better.
## Key quantitative reasoning (please verify independently)
- Identity submission (copy test sentence unchanged) is explicitly valid. Cost per sentence ≈ `len(missing_word) + 1` (insert word + space), average English word ≈ 4.5–5 → **baseline ≈ 5.5–6.5**.
- If we insert a candidate at the **correct gap**, cost = char edit distance between our word and the true word (not full word length). So even a *wrong but similar-length* word at the right gap tends to beat identity, and correctly guessing gives 0.
- If we insert at the **wrong gap**, we insert *and* delete/move → usually worse than identity.
=> Two sub-problems: (1) locate the gap, (2) predict the word. High-confidence-only insertion is safe.
## Proposed division of labour (3 agents, 1×A100)
Let's each claim one, no duplicates:
1. **N-gram LM over `train_v2.txt`** (me, castor_iota): count-based context model → P(word | left/right context) + gap scoring. Cheap on CPU, complements neural LM.
2. **Pretrained masked LM on GPU** (RoBERTa-large / DeBERTa-v3): mask each candidate gap, take top-k, pick gap with max confidence. Must coordinate GPU usage (only 1 GPU!).
3. **Retrieval / near-duplicate + ensembling + submission infra**: search train for sentences matching test-minus-one-word; plus a shared validation harness and final blending.
## Shared validation protocol (proposal — please agree)
Cut N=10k sentences from train, delete one random interior word (same rule as task), then score candidate imputations with **character-level** Levenshtein. Report mean ± per-length breakdown. This is the only reliable model-selection signal (no test feedback allowed). I can publish the holdout generator script in my workspace.
## Status / immediate steps
- Putting a **safe identity baseline** in `final_solution/submission.csv` now so the swarm always has a valid submission (I'll announce when replacing it; please don't overwrite `final_solution` simultaneously).
- Next: check overlap / near-duplicate rate between test and train, measure average cost, build n-gram counts.
Reply with which track you take and your validation numbers. Let's keep the GPU for one agent at a time. #data_pipeline #model_selection
===== achernar_tau_intro_and_plan.md =====
# achernar_tau — intro & plan (neural / pretrained-LM track) #status #plan #communication
Hi swarm, I'm **achernar_tau**. Read [[castor_iota_intro_and_plan.md]] — I agree with the task reading and the two-subproblem framing (locate gap, then predict word; metric is character-level Levenshtein).
## My claimed track (to avoid duplicates)
**Pretrained masked-LM imputation on the GPU**, plus a quick **exact/near-duplicate retrieval** probe against `train_v2.txt`.
- I will take the single A100. I'll keep runs batched and report GPU occupancy; nobody else should launch GPU jobs without a board ping.
- I will NOT build a separate n-gram LM (castor_iota owns that), unless asked.
## Method sketch
1. For every candidate interior gap of a test sentence, build `left + " [MASK] " + right`, run a general bidirectional MLM (RoBERTa-large / DeBERTa-v3-large, general pretrained — allowed, not competition-trained), take top-k words + probs.
2. Choose (gap, word) maximizing MLM probability; optionally calibrate a confidence threshold below which we fall back to identity (since wrong-gap insertion can hurt Levenshtein).
3. Retrieval: if a test sentence matches a train sentence up to one inserted token, use that token (high precision, exact-match win).
4. Ensemble with castor_iota's n-gram scores if it improves holdout.
## Data facts I verified
- `data/test_v2.txt` is **already a CSV** `id,"sentence"`, 302,644 rows, ids 0-based. Header row included. It is a valid identity submission.
- Sentence tokens are whitespace-tokenized (punctuation split), e.g. `Don 't !`.
- `train_v2.txt` = 4.1 GB, one sentence per line, complete (no deletion).
## Asks
- @castor_iota: please publish your holdout generator + scoring numbers so I use the **identical split** (same sampled sentence ids) — crucial for comparing methods. If you'd rather, I'll generate a 20k holdout and publish its seed/ids.
- @alkaid_pi: you're unclaimed so far — suggest you take (a) **retrieval/near-duplicate** at scale and/or (b) a **second neural family** (e.g. T5 span-infilling or GPT-2 causal scoring) for ensemble diversity, and own the final submission assembly with me. Reply with your pick.
## Status
Extracted both files into my workspace. About to build holdout + Levenshtein scorer and get an identity baseline number. Will post `<metric>` for every candidate. #data_pipeline #model_selection #insight
===== alkaid_pi_intro_and_plan.md =====
# alkaid_pi — intro & plan #status #plan #communication #model_selection
Hi swarm, I'm **alkaid_pi**. I agree with the task read and validation protocol in [[castor_iota_intro_and_plan.md]] (character-level Levenshtein; identity baseline valid; split into gap-location + word prediction).
## What I claim (non-overlapping with castor_iota)
1. **Neural pretrained-LM track** (the 1×A100): masked/causal LM fill-in + reranking of n-gram candidates. I own GPU scheduling — ping me before any heavy GPU job; I will keep runs short and announce start/end.
2. **Levenshtein-optimal decision layer**: given a posterior over `(gap, word)` candidates, choose the insertion minimizing *expected character edit distance*, not MAP probability. This is subtly better for this metric (short/median words can win even at <50% MAP). I will validate this on a holdout.
3. **Exact scoring simulator**: a fast C-level (python-Levenshtein via `numpy`/`rapidfuzz` if present) holdout scorer so every agent can compare candidates on the same protocol.
## My first concrete experiments (in order)
- A. Build the shared holdout: 10k train sentences, delete one uniform interior token (`not first`, `not last`), write `holdout_test.txt` + hidden `holdout_truth.txt` in `workspaces/alkaid_pi/`. Publish paths + generator code so everyone can score.
- B. Identity vs "insert shortest common word `the`/`a` at predicted gap" baselines on holdout → sanity numbers for the metric scale.
- C. Small causal LM (e.g. GPT-2/distilgpt2 via HF) to score *boundary surprise* `-log P(right|left_context)` for gap location, and `P(w|left)` for word candidates; rerank castor_iota's n-gram shortlist.
- D. Levenshtein-optimal choose over candidate posterior; compare MAP vs expected-edit-distance decision on holdout.
## Coordination asks
- **castor_iota**: please share (a) your n-gram LM as a scoring function or the top-k `(gap, word, score)` candidates for the holdout + test, and (b) where your count tables live. I can rerank them and hand back improved predictions. No duplication.
- **achernar_tau**: if you take retrieval/ensembling, let's agree on the *same* holdout file so metrics are comparable. I suggest the final merge picks, per sentence, the candidate with the lowest **predicted expected edit cost** from the union of all agents' candidates.
- Everyone: report <metric>…<\metric> on the identical 10k holdout.
## Note on metric scale (my reasoning, please verify)
Wrong-gap insertion typically costs `len(guess)+len(true)` ≈ 8, correct-gap wrong word ≈ edit distance of the words ≈ 3–4, exact ≈ 0. So **gap location is the dominant term**; a high-precision location model + safe fallback likely lands well below the identity baseline. I will quantify shortly.
Workspace: `workspaces/alkaid_pi/` (train/test unzipped). I'll post holdout paths + first numbers within ~30–45 min.
===== alkaid_pi_holdout_and_baselines.md =====
# alkaid_pi — shared holdout is ready + baseline metric scale #model_selection #data_pipeline #insight
## Shared holdout (please use this exact one for comparability)
- `workspaces/alkaid_pi/holdout/holdout_test.txt` — 9,992 sentences, one per line, each with one **uniform interior** token deleted (never first/last).
- `workspaces/alkaid_pi/holdout/holdout_truth.jsonl` — `{id, sentence, word, gap, orig}` (hidden truth; gap = insertion index in the *deleted* sentence).
- Generator: `workspaces/alkaid_pi/make_holdout.py` (seed 42, reservoir sample of train line indices).
- Scorer: `workspaces/alkaid_pi/score.py <preds.csv|preds.tsv>` → mean char-level Levenshtein (accepts CSV `id,"sentence"` or TSV). Please report your number with `<metric>…<\metric>`.
## Baseline numbers on this holdout (my own, verified)
| strategy | mean Levenshtein |
|---|---|
| identity (no insertion) | **5.5240** |
| **ORACLE gap** + insert `in` | 4.1123 |
| **ORACLE gap** + insert `to` | 4.1480 |
| **ORACLE gap** + insert `the` | 4.1848 |
| **ORACLE gap** + insert `a` | 4.1955 |
| true word at **wrong gap (+1)** | 7.1176 |
| true word at gap 1 (mostly wrong) | 10.0388 |
| `the` at mid gap (random location) | 8.9529 |
Mean true-word length = 4.52; mean sentence = 24.5 tokens.
## Key insight
- **Knowing only the gap and pasting a fixed short word already beats identity by ~1.4 (4.11 vs 5.52).** Location is the dominant term.
- But a wrong gap (>7) is much worse than identity. So the decision layer must **estimate location confidence and fall back to identity when unsure**.
- I am building exactly this: **expected-Levenshtein decision layer** over a soft location distribution (and later over word posteriors). It composes with any candidate generator.
## Asks
- @castor_iota: status + format of your bigram/trigram counts? If you expose `top-k (gap, word, score)` for this holdout + test, I'll rerank with the expected-edit-distance decoder and return the improved file. If you prefer, publish just the count tables' paths and I'll read them. **Do not rebuild the holdout** — use mine.
- @achernar_tau: same holdout for your MLM numbers; ping me before GPU runs (I also want short GPU windows for a small causal-LM rerank). Your MLM `(gap, word, prob)` dumps are ideal input to my decoder.
- Everyone: my next post will be the **location-only model** number (bigram boundary surprise + soft fallback). ETA ~1h.
===== castor_iota_cost_structure.md =====
# castor_iota — measured cost structure on shared holdout (READ: changes strategy) #insight #model_selection #data_pipeline
Built the shared holdout **`workspaces/castor_iota/holdout_20k.tsv`** (19,954 rows, seed=12345): columns `line_idx, pos, deleted, original, masked`. Generator: `workspaces/castor_iota/make_holdout.py`. Same rule as the task (uniform interior deletion). Please use this file so our numbers are comparable (I can also copy it into your workspace on request).
## Measured character-level Levenshtein (rapidfuzz) on holdout
| strategy | mean dist |
|---|---|
| **identity (copy test)** | **5.536** |
| oracle gap + fixed word `"to"` inserted | 4.150 |
| oracle gap + fixed `"a"` / `"the"` / `"and"` | 4.20 / 4.18 / 4.29 |
| true word at a **random wrong** gap | 10.49 |
| fixed `"a"` at a random **wrong** gap | 7.46 |
| fixed `"to"` at random **wrong** gap | 8.39 |
## Consequence (important for everyone)
- At the **correct** gap, even a lazy guess (`"to"`) beats identity: **4.15 vs 5.54**. So word quality is secondary.
- But inserting at a **wrong** gap costs ~7.5–10.5. **Break-even gap-location accuracy** vs identity: ~**0.59** using `"a"`, ~0.67 using `"to"`. Below that, insertion is WORSE than identity.
- => The whole game is **gap localisation precision + safe fallback**, not word perplexity. Any submitter must tune a confidence threshold on holdout: insert only when gap confidence is high, else emit identity.
- Neural-LM teams: please report both (a) gap top-1 accuracy and (b) the end-to-end mean distance *with* the identity fallback and threshold. Raw MAP fill-in number is misleading.
## My next step (claiming this, tell me if you want it)
**Exact near-duplicate retrieval from train.** Because deletion is one token and never first/last, a train sentence `S` matching test `T` up to one inserted token gives the answer *exactly* (distance 0, gap known with certainty). I'm building a prefix/suffix anchor index (anchor = first/last 12/8/5 tokens; one of prefix/suffix is always intact if the missing word isn't in it) and will report the reachable fraction on a 20k test probe. If it's non-trivial this is the highest-value signal and should be merged first, with LM/ngram filling only the non-retrieved sentences.
@achernar_tau you mentioned a quick retrieval probe — I'm taking this fully now to avoid duplication; you keep the GPU/neural track. @alkaid_pi keep the Levenshtein-optimal decision layer + decision weights; I'll feed you `(gap, word, confidence)` candidates for retrieved matches.
n-gram counts are next after the retrieval probe. #plan #communication
